WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.126 --> 00:00:02.709
(upbeat music)

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:08.970
- Welcome back to another
episode of the AddyHour.

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:10.740
As always, it's my pleasure to be here

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:11.880
with all of you as listeners,

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.830
and have continued to really be encouraged

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:17.640
by all of the feedback that
you all have been sending in.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.380
So today I'm really excited to continue

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:21.210
on our theme on so many different levels.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:22.830
This is really a pinnacle in a sense,

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:23.760
'cause we're gonna integrate

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.200
across many of the different things

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.930
that we're really passionate
about here on the AddyHour.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:28.860
And today I'm honored

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:31.860
to be able to welcome Dr.
Stephanie Pinder-Amaker

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.680
for conversation about diversity, equity,

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:38.880
inclusion, and mental health
on campuses and in society.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.040
So obviously that's a
very encompassing title,

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.930
but I think, as you all will hear,

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.390
this is very appropriate
for the work that she does.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:49.350
So just to give her a brief
introduction, Dr. Pinder-Amaker

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:52.440
is McLean Hospital's Inaugural
Chief Diversity Equity

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:53.940
and Inclusion Officer.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:55.170
She's the Founding Director

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.840
of McLean's College Mental Health Program.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.880
She's an Assistant Professor of Psychology

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:01.440
in Harvard Medical School,

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.430
and she's a Consulting Psychologist
for the Boston Celtics.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:07.440
So, many different roles as you can hear.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.810
And we will also touch on the whole aspect

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:12.960
of balancing and juggling in
this conversation as well,

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.120
partially selfishly, because
I get that question a lot too,

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.130
and it's always helpfully
hear others talk through

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:19.290
that aspect of their journey.

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:20.820
But in terms of, again,
the intro introduction

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:23.850
to Dr. Pinder-Amaker, she
is someone who is guided

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:25.440
by the models of cultural humility

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.030
and shared responsibility,

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:29.310
and she's passionate
about moving organizations

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:31.200
beyond diversity by the numbers

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.260
towards sustainable,
inclusivity, and promoting

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.500
culturally responsive
mental health approaches.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.230
She's consulted for dozens of universities

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:43.470
and secondary schools, also
sport teams and nonprofits

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:46.350
to really develop interventions
at the intersection

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:50.070
of social/cultural
identity and mental health.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:52.020
Her new book, "Did That Just Happen?!

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.310
Beyond Diversity,

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:56.040
Creating Inclusive and
Sustainable Organizations,"

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.860
has been selected as a
community reads choice

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:00.840
by colleges and corporations,

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.690
and has also been nominated
for several national awards.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:06.450
And that's a book we'll be
talking about today as well.

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:08.850
Along with her co-author
Dr. Lauren Wadsworth,

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.950
she actually serves, of course,

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:12.930
on several advisory boards as well

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:14.430
to several different organizations

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:17.700
that committed to social
justice and health equity.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.050
I should also mention that we share

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:21.660
a couple of different alma maters as well.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.340
Dr. Pinder-Amaker graduated

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.290
from Duke University with her BS,

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:27.120
then went on to do a
doctorate in psychology

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.650
from Vanderbilt University,

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.020
and then did pre-doctoral
and post-doctoral fellowships

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:32.310
at the Yale School of Medicine

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:35.040
and at Duke Medical School respectively.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:39.210
So someone that I have some
mutual background with,

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:40.950
so to speak, and it's
my pleasure and honor

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:43.550
to be able to welcome her
here to the podcast today.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:45.570
- Thank you so much.

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:49.740
It is my pleasure and my
honor to be here with you

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:51.903
at the AddyHour.

00:02:52.788 --> 00:02:54.750
- Wonderful.
- And just wanna say,

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.180
to be healthy and whole,

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.970
one must approach things holistically.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:00.930
I love that.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.000
- Yep. Yep.
- Thank you for that.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.550
- Of course, you're in the right place,

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:08.310
I mean, obviously that's in our essence,

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:11.100
but even just hearing you speak
that is so just encouraging,

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.110
as I can tell that it
resonates with you as well.

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.030
So definitely looking
forward to being able

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:16.320
to have this conversation.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:18.180
And I've really appreciate the ways

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:19.140
that we've been able to connect

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:20.220
over the past couple of years,

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:23.250
even before we start this
podcast and just the insights

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:24.960
that I've been able to
gleam from your work.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.840
And so in some ways this
is full circle for me

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.030
to be able to introduce you
to our listeners as well.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:31.435
So appreciate you being here.

00:03:31.435 --> 00:03:32.973
- Thank you, thank you.

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:36.030
- And of course, as you know,
and as my listeners know,

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:38.070
I always just like to
start with a check-in,

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:40.680
especially with everything that
we're continuing to navigate

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:41.970
as a society.

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:43.530
And some ways it feels like,

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:47.580
because things are so
heavy and challenging,

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:49.200
it's almost surreal in a sense

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:50.430
that just talking to different people,

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:52.470
it seems like some people are
really weighed down by it,

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:55.410
some people are trying not to
pay attention to it as much

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:56.790
because it's too much,

00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:58.920
some people are kind of
in between those two.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:00.750
So I would just be curious to hear,

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:01.770
especially in the work that you do,

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:03.930
how you've just been navigating

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:06.060
through life on a daily
basis at this point

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:08.520
as you tried to balance
what you do with your work

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:09.810
and then just navigating everything

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:12.870
that so many of us are
experiencing in society?

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.100
- Such a good question.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:16.323
Thank you for checking in on that.

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:21.270
Doing pretty well right now.

00:04:21.270 --> 00:04:25.320
Certainly better I think now
than this time a year ago

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:28.200
and better than the year
before that at this time.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:31.110
So I feel like trending
in the right direction

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:33.540
and that's important.

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:38.540
As you mentioned, it's been
an extremely demanding time

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:44.193
to be in both the fields of
mental health service delivery,

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:48.450
as well as promoting
diversity, equity, inclusion,

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:50.520
and belonging sort of across the board.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:55.520
So I feel like I've learned
a lot in the past few years

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:00.750
and really had to put into
practice new strategies

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:05.940
for maintaining balance

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:09.090
to keep it all going
in the right direction.

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:11.023
- Yeah, that makes sense.
- So a work in progress.

00:05:11.023 --> 00:05:14.160
- Yeah, yeah, I think
that's a helpful answer

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:15.210
in so many ways,

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:17.760
to even know that someone
like you in your position

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:22.590
who is practicing it for others
and trying to instill those

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:24.660
and still working through
that yourself too.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:25.493
Would you be willing to share

00:05:25.493 --> 00:05:27.450
what some of those new practices have been

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:28.680
for you, personally?

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:29.993
- Yeah, sure.

00:05:29.993 --> 00:05:33.240
One, interestingly enough,

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:38.240
we started a new practice
with our staff meetings.

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:41.250
Before we jump into the agenda,

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:45.630
we actually have a check in,
like a personal check in,

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:47.550
and I can't take credit for that.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.590
It was like another staff member

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:51.420
who said this would be really important

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:52.803
for us to do right now.

00:05:54.329 --> 00:05:56.640
And so that's been helpful.

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:58.650
A few things that I practiced

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:00.210
that became really important to me

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:05.210
during the height of the initial
surge of the pandemic was,

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:11.700
I guess to say it plainly,

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:14.460
to begin practicing what I preach.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:17.370
As a mental health provider,
I do so much speaking

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:19.770
and talking and teaching about self-care,

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:24.330
but really had to push
myself to put those practices

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:28.200
into place and maintain them for myself

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:31.710
and to hold myself accountable for that.

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:33.543
One of the practices that I started doing,

00:06:33.543 --> 00:06:36.150
I do a lot of talking and speaking about,

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:37.860
resilience, for example.

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:42.300
And so I created for
teaching it, this pneumonic

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:45.570
for remembering some of the
evidence based components

00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:47.580
that promote resiliency.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:51.630
So I'm still working on,
this is hard to say verbally,

00:06:51.630 --> 00:06:53.700
but the way that I see it in my mind is,

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:56.850
I think about a title resilience

00:06:56.850 --> 00:07:00.330
and then the number six/S.

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:02.853
So resilience 6/s.

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:08.910
But the six S's, that's how
I remember the components.

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:13.350
And what I do for myself is
do like a scan of those areas

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:17.190
to think about how I'm
doing across those areas.

00:07:17.190 --> 00:07:19.980
And if I'm feeling sort
of unmoored or off balance

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:24.180
or out of sorts, then I do that
scan across those six areas

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:27.870
to see where I might
be letting myself down.

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:29.490
And it's something that I've been,

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:31.800
like I said, practicing
and teaching for years,

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:36.090
but had to really begin
implementing it for myself.

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:37.710
Do you wanna know what the six S's are?

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:38.635
- Yeah, I'm very curious.

00:07:38.635 --> 00:07:40.380
(both laughs)

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:42.153
- So, structure,

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:46.110
which I don't typically
have a challenge with that,

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:48.270
but a lot of people did have a challenge

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:51.750
with maintaining structure
during the pandemic.

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:55.020
So, structure, self-care, which is huge,

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:57.960
and spirituality, those
are the first three Ss.

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:01.200
And then the last three
are sense of purpose,

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:04.320
sense of belonging, and sense of humor.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:08.070
So I'll sort of do a
personal scan of those areas

00:08:08.070 --> 00:08:12.210
to sort of figure out what
needs some bolstering.

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:13.410
And it's been really helpful.

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:17.280
And the other strategy I'm
happy to share is so basic.

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:18.993
It is just taking a walk,

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:22.350
but a specific kind of walk.

00:08:22.350 --> 00:08:24.750
I've read about it in the New York Times.

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:26.400
They called it an "awe walk."

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:29.940
I was doing it before
I saw the title of it.

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:33.930
And all it is, is getting
outdoors and just taking a walk

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:35.550
and trying to discover something

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:37.800
that you've never seen before.

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:39.840
Just something you could be in awe of.

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:42.870
And so pretty basic,
but it's been helping.

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:43.703
- That's great.

00:08:43.703 --> 00:08:47.017
I mean, basic but powerful
in so many ways, too.

00:08:47.017 --> 00:08:48.060
And I mean, so many things

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:49.860
of what you mentioned really resonated,

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:51.240
just practicing what you preached.

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:52.650
I can think of so many instances

00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:56.010
where I verbalized some of
those things in certain settings

00:08:56.010 --> 00:08:57.570
and then had people respond and say,

00:08:57.570 --> 00:08:59.700
oh, that really resonated
with me when I heard you talk

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:02.730
about just having to
reset your expectations

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:04.560
and know that you might be in a place

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:06.300
just because of everything that
was happening with pandemic

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:07.710
or, otherwise, that you might not be able

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:08.940
to hit those goals,

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:11.640
and to be able to say "that's
fine and take a step back."

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:13.770
So just to hear that you've
been verbalizing some

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:14.730
of those things as well.

00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:17.700
And then as you mentioned
maintaining, that's huge.

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:21.030
'Cause talking to so many
people who had good intentions

00:09:21.030 --> 00:09:22.530
but weren't able to maintain that,

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:23.940
but then were able to do that

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:25.170
through connecting with other people

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:27.390
or having people hold them accountable.

00:09:27.390 --> 00:09:29.640
So I think that's amazing.

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:31.710
And it's funny, because as
you mentioned the walking,

00:09:31.710 --> 00:09:32.850
I even was thinking back,

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:35.370
I shared this with people
in my research group to do,

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:36.420
the beginning of the pandemic,

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:39.870
just being more specific
about taking some daily walks

00:09:39.870 --> 00:09:42.030
or few times a week and working out more

00:09:42.030 --> 00:09:44.190
and somehow that just
kind of slipped away.

00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:47.430
So that maintenance part
definitely wasn't there,

00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:49.830
but to be able to even verbalize
that and have people say,

00:09:49.830 --> 00:09:51.657
okay, I also feel like I couldn't keep up,

00:09:51.657 --> 00:09:53.580
and that helps them kind of move along,

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:55.530
but so many aspects of what you share

00:09:55.530 --> 00:09:58.170
I think are so powerful,
simple and powerful.

00:09:58.170 --> 00:09:59.520
So I know that people listening,

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:01.320
even hearing those six S's

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:04.597
will be able to put some
of those things in place.

00:10:04.597 --> 00:10:07.260
- Yeah, and I feel like the six S's,

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:11.070
resilient 6s really aligns very nicely

00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:13.890
with to remain healthy

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:16.710
and whole one must approach holistically.

00:10:16.710 --> 00:10:18.450
And so it kind of think

00:10:18.450 --> 00:10:21.003
about the whole sort
of realm of your life.

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:25.350
Because it can't, at least for me,

00:10:25.350 --> 00:10:28.710
it can't be just one
intervention or one strategy.

00:10:28.710 --> 00:10:31.110
I really need to think holistically.

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:34.323
This past weekend, for example,

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:39.180
I went to an outdoor concert,
an outdoor jazz concert,

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:44.070
first concert I'd been
to in over three years,

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:47.970
and it just really fed my soul.

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:51.810
It was wonderful to be back
in community with people,

00:10:51.810 --> 00:10:53.313
but also outdoors.

00:10:54.211 --> 00:10:55.260
- Yeah, that's true.

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:56.850
And I imagine you probably felt the music

00:10:56.850 --> 00:10:57.805
in a different way, too.

00:10:57.805 --> 00:10:59.610
- I really did, I really did.

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:01.380
- [Both] Yeah, yeah.

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:02.213
- That's so good.

00:11:02.213 --> 00:11:03.046
Well, I know that people are listening

00:11:03.046 --> 00:11:04.950
are gonna be encouraged
even by that already.

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:06.630
And then you mentioned holistically,

00:11:06.630 --> 00:11:08.100
I mean, as you were
sharing those six steps,

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:09.960
I was thinking there's a
lot of discipline there,

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:11.490
but there's also aspects of hope

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:13.170
and thinking about
ourselves as whole beings

00:11:13.170 --> 00:11:14.670
as you talked about the spiritual side,

00:11:14.670 --> 00:11:16.470
as you talked about humor.

00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:18.120
So you out to be able to have those things

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:20.490
that really pick us up
and just give us life.

00:11:20.490 --> 00:11:22.650
So I'm impressed already.

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:24.180
And I also appreciate the honesty

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:26.010
about saying that it's a work in progress.

00:11:26.010 --> 00:11:28.710
So I know it's not perfected by any means.

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:31.530
- Yes, it's definitely a work in progress.

00:11:31.530 --> 00:11:36.060
And I think that in the
early stages of the pandemic,

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:39.300
I mean, to be like fully transparent

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:42.240
at moving to working remotely,

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:44.730
what happened to me is what
happened to so many people,

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:48.030
is that my Workday really extended.

00:11:48.030 --> 00:11:51.090
Without the commute on both ends,

00:11:51.090 --> 00:11:54.960
I just jumped in earlier
and I was being so efficient

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:56.490
and getting so much done

00:11:56.490 --> 00:11:58.983
and feeling really great about myself.

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:05.730
And then about maybe 6,
7, 8 months into that,

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:09.270
really had to stop and
observe what was happening

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:14.270
and how I had really lost some
degree of work/life balance.

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:18.360
And it's just really important
to take note of that,

00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:19.193
to recognize when it's,

00:12:19.193 --> 00:12:21.200
or at least to have someone check you.

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:22.860
In my case, I did have someone.

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:26.437
And that was my spouse,
my husband, who said,

00:12:26.437 --> 00:12:30.064
"maybe we're gonna stop
bringing the laptop to bed."

00:12:30.064 --> 00:12:32.400
(both laughs)

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:34.267
And at that moment, we both said,

00:12:34.267 --> 00:12:36.300
"let's talk about what we've been doing

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:38.010
and figure out how to reign this in

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:41.250
and how to help support
each other in that regard."

00:12:41.250 --> 00:12:42.240
- That's so good.

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:44.760
Yeah, to have someone who's
willing to call you out,

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:47.280
but then also to walk it out
with you at the same time.

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:48.660
- Call you out, walk it out.

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:49.560
I like that.

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:50.940
- Yeah. That's great.

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:51.990
- I'm gonna use that.

00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:55.183
- Yeah. Feel free.
(both laugh)

00:12:55.183 --> 00:12:56.016
- I'll credit you.

00:12:56.016 --> 00:12:57.000
I'll credit you.

00:12:57.000 --> 00:12:58.920
- Either way is fine.

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:00.480
Now that you brought it to my attention,

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:01.947
I'm gonna use it as well.

00:13:01.947 --> 00:13:04.080
(both laugh)

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:05.229
It works for both of us.

00:13:05.229 --> 00:13:06.390
- Good. Good.

00:13:06.390 --> 00:13:08.700
- But, I mean, that's really
encouraging to hear it too.

00:13:08.700 --> 00:13:09.750
On that same note,

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:11.430
'cause, I mean, now that we're
gonna use this call it out,

00:13:11.430 --> 00:13:14.220
work it out or moniker
we just came up with.

00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:17.280
I'm curious, within the
College Mental Health Program

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:19.020
that you've been leading,

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:20.760
one, if you could talk
a little bit about that

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:22.410
and then also talk about

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:25.290
what you've had to do within
that realm within the pandemic,

00:13:25.290 --> 00:13:26.970
because all these things
that you've mentioned

00:13:26.970 --> 00:13:29.310
that many of us have been struggling with,

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:32.370
and especially if there's
mental illness involved,

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:34.260
I'm curious how that has
also impacted the work

00:13:34.260 --> 00:13:35.093
that you're doing,

00:13:35.093 --> 00:13:37.080
but maybe just to start
to give listeners a sense

00:13:37.080 --> 00:13:38.550
of what that program looks like.

00:13:38.550 --> 00:13:40.440
- Sure, sure.

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:42.300
So the College Mental Health Program

00:13:42.300 --> 00:13:45.600
is somewhat of a unique model

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:48.930
because we're a hospital-based

00:13:48.930 --> 00:13:50.640
College Mental Health Program,

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:54.000
which, in itself, is a little atypical.

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:55.710
Usually these programs are based

00:13:55.710 --> 00:13:58.350
within institutions of higher education.

00:13:58.350 --> 00:14:01.550
But the reason that we
decided to develop the program

00:14:01.550 --> 00:14:06.550
is that although we work
in a hospital setting,

00:14:07.410 --> 00:14:10.740
so not situated directly
on the college campus,

00:14:10.740 --> 00:14:13.050
each year in our hospital system,

00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:18.050
we see college students from
over 200 different institutions

00:14:18.870 --> 00:14:21.000
of higher education annually.

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:23.280
And that's just an
extraordinary statistic.

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:26.280
I mean, it still blows my
mind when I think about it,

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:28.530
but I think it's helpful
to sort of start with that

00:14:28.530 --> 00:14:30.750
because it really is that statistic

00:14:30.750 --> 00:14:32.823
that drives the work that we do,

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:36.900
because it is such a
unique sort of demographic.

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:39.420
And so we focus on three areas.

00:14:39.420 --> 00:14:44.160
We deliver student-focused care,

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:48.330
strategies that really focus
on the student identity

00:14:48.330 --> 00:14:50.700
and integrate those strategies

00:14:50.700 --> 00:14:52.590
into existing treatment models.

00:14:52.590 --> 00:14:57.210
And so we work with students
at all levels of care,

00:14:57.210 --> 00:15:00.330
and for your listeners, what
that means specifically is,

00:15:00.330 --> 00:15:03.060
wherever students might seek
mental health treatment,

00:15:03.060 --> 00:15:05.070
whether it's at the
inpatient level of care,

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:07.410
which is the highest level of care,

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:09.210
or in a day treatment setting,

00:15:09.210 --> 00:15:11.490
where someone comes consecutively

00:15:11.490 --> 00:15:13.110
for a period of weeks each day

00:15:13.110 --> 00:15:16.200
to do a little intensive outpatient work,

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:19.410
residential, students in
residential treatment,

00:15:19.410 --> 00:15:22.350
who are in treatment and
living on the premises,

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:23.400
and then outpatient.

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:24.660
So we see students

00:15:24.660 --> 00:15:26.070
across all those levels of care,

00:15:26.070 --> 00:15:30.120
presenting with the whole
range of psychiatric diagnoses.

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:33.330
But we specifically
integrate a student focus

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:34.473
into that work.

00:15:35.970 --> 00:15:39.840
And because we see such a
broad number of students,

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:40.800
a large number of students

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:44.160
from such a broad number of
colleges and universities,

00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:47.820
not only do we deliver
clinical treatment work

00:15:47.820 --> 00:15:48.843
and do a lot of,

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:53.460
basically just trying to
help folks think about

00:15:53.460 --> 00:15:55.620
how to support the mental health needs

00:15:55.620 --> 00:15:57.630
of students more effectively

00:15:57.630 --> 00:15:59.810
by taking into
consideration the fact that,

00:15:59.810 --> 00:16:03.150
this sounds so obvious,
but that they are students.

00:16:03.150 --> 00:16:04.710
And so why does that matter?

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:05.820
Why is that so pivotal?

00:16:05.820 --> 00:16:09.510
Which typically does not always happen.

00:16:09.510 --> 00:16:11.430
So there's a clinical piece,

00:16:11.430 --> 00:16:13.200
and then we do research.

00:16:13.200 --> 00:16:15.480
That's another sort of
domain that we work in it.

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:17.970
And the easiest way to describe that is,

00:16:17.970 --> 00:16:21.180
as we're developing new
strategies and interventions,

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:24.540
things that we think are improving

00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:26.040
the mental health outcomes

00:16:26.040 --> 00:16:27.990
for the students with whom we're working,

00:16:27.990 --> 00:16:29.610
we're also evaluating that

00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:32.310
because we don't wanna just guess at it

00:16:32.310 --> 00:16:35.160
or say we think this is
working or we suspect this.

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:37.800
We wanna have the evidence
and document that evidence.

00:16:37.800 --> 00:16:39.960
And we've been able to
do that over the years.

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:44.460
Be able to show that when you
integrate the sort of focus

00:16:44.460 --> 00:16:47.820
on student identity into
traditional models of care,

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:52.380
like in our outpatient, excuse
me, our day treatment setting

00:16:52.380 --> 00:16:56.670
that the students actually do
better in terms of their level

00:16:56.670 --> 00:16:59.280
of reducing levels of
depression and anxiety

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:02.460
when that sort of focus has
been integrated into care.

00:17:02.460 --> 00:17:04.140
So there's the treatment piece,

00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:05.580
there's the research piece.

00:17:05.580 --> 00:17:07.170
Is this working, is it not?

00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:10.830
And if not, let's figure out what does.

00:17:10.830 --> 00:17:13.650
And then a really big piece of the work,

00:17:13.650 --> 00:17:15.570
which is constantly evolving,

00:17:15.570 --> 00:17:17.550
centers on the fact that we see students

00:17:17.550 --> 00:17:18.780
from all over the country,

00:17:18.780 --> 00:17:22.680
and that piece is we engage
colleges and universities.

00:17:22.680 --> 00:17:27.600
So we provide consultation
services to schools,

00:17:27.600 --> 00:17:30.900
and, essentially, really
strengthening the relationship

00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:33.960
between the work that we do
and the work that they're doing

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:38.280
and constantly putting our
heads together to figure out

00:17:38.280 --> 00:17:41.710
what's working for the
students that we actually share

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:44.910
and what's not working and what's needed.

00:17:44.910 --> 00:17:49.260
And so we develop new
strategies, new interventions,

00:17:49.260 --> 00:17:51.600
new services in partnership,

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:55.320
and then collaborate, new
policies, new programming

00:17:55.320 --> 00:17:58.440
with colleges and universities
all over the country.

00:17:58.440 --> 00:18:01.020
And so that's the sort of the full span

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:02.430
of the work that we do.

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:04.590
So we're not,

00:18:04.590 --> 00:18:07.290
our work isn't just situated
where the hospital is

00:18:07.290 --> 00:18:08.520
in Belmont, Massachusetts.

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:11.790
We spend a lot of time visiting campuses

00:18:11.790 --> 00:18:15.570
and engaging with students
and staff and faculty

00:18:15.570 --> 00:18:18.543
and mental health providers
on college campuses as well.

00:18:19.710 --> 00:18:21.810
- I mean, it sounds so
comprehensive and so important

00:18:21.810 --> 00:18:22.650
on so many fronts,

00:18:22.650 --> 00:18:25.710
the way that you're really
integrating across those pieces.

00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:27.060
again, meet people where they are,

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:28.830
which we've talked a lot
about on this podcast,

00:18:28.830 --> 00:18:31.560
but then also be informed
by whether the things

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:35.160
and the practices and
interventions are effective or not,

00:18:35.160 --> 00:18:36.210
but then also partnering

00:18:36.210 --> 00:18:38.970
with so many different entities as well.

00:18:38.970 --> 00:18:40.260
I'm curious as you've done that,

00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:41.970
'cause you even talked about policy.

00:18:41.970 --> 00:18:44.460
How did you make inroads into that?

00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:46.800
Because it sounds like
there's a level of trust

00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:48.570
that has to also develop there,

00:18:48.570 --> 00:18:50.430
one, to be able to listen
and try and work together,

00:18:50.430 --> 00:18:52.500
and especially if that's
gonna move towards policy

00:18:52.500 --> 00:18:53.333
on campuses.

00:18:53.333 --> 00:18:55.580
So how has that piece come about for you

00:18:55.580 --> 00:18:57.273
to actually be effective?

00:18:58.440 --> 00:19:02.970
- Dr. Addy, you took the words out of,

00:19:02.970 --> 00:19:04.170
are you reading my mind?

00:19:05.130 --> 00:19:08.160
The most important thing
and the very first thing

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:10.650
that we had to do in
developing the program

00:19:10.650 --> 00:19:14.670
was create a level of trust,

00:19:14.670 --> 00:19:16.770
'cause you can't just make this stuff up.

00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:18.330
And as you know,

00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:21.240
you can't just show up on campuses

00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:24.933
an an expert in anything, for that matter.

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:27.930
But you have to figure out

00:19:27.930 --> 00:19:30.720
how to strengthen the relationship.

00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:34.260
And so we went about that
in a very intentional way

00:19:34.260 --> 00:19:37.170
from the outset, do a number of things,

00:19:37.170 --> 00:19:39.783
an example, a number of processes,

00:19:40.740 --> 00:19:44.700
one example is we created
this range of programming

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:48.900
that we titled "McLean Host"
and then fill in the blank.

00:19:48.900 --> 00:19:52.530
And so we started reaching out to,

00:19:52.530 --> 00:19:55.500
there are like 75 to 80 colleges

00:19:55.500 --> 00:19:59.310
and universities just within a
close radius of the hospital.

00:19:59.310 --> 00:20:00.780
So it's a good place to be able

00:20:00.780 --> 00:20:02.970
to build a College Mental Health Program.

00:20:02.970 --> 00:20:06.450
But we started reaching
out and inviting folks

00:20:06.450 --> 00:20:08.433
from campus to visit our campus.

00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:12.780
And that was one way of
strengthen the relationship,

00:20:12.780 --> 00:20:14.340
getting them from there,

00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:16.560
from where they sit on
their campus to where we,

00:20:16.560 --> 00:20:18.660
and our hospital setting also looks

00:20:18.660 --> 00:20:21.300
like a small college campus,
the way that it's situated.

00:20:21.300 --> 00:20:23.790
It's not like in a physical building,

00:20:23.790 --> 00:20:28.790
like a single structure, but
really spread out over land.

00:20:28.920 --> 00:20:32.430
And we engage them and
ask them to think about,

00:20:32.430 --> 00:20:35.460
when they came to visit us,
what they wanted to explore

00:20:35.460 --> 00:20:37.860
about how we are working with students.

00:20:37.860 --> 00:20:40.260
So taking them through an abbreviated tour

00:20:40.260 --> 00:20:42.030
of some of the work that we were doing

00:20:42.030 --> 00:20:43.350
and just kind of building,

00:20:43.350 --> 00:20:45.060
getting to know each other basically.

00:20:45.060 --> 00:20:46.440
And we had,

00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:49.260
one would think of as like an
exchange program, if you will,

00:20:49.260 --> 00:20:50.970
where then a reciprocal,

00:20:50.970 --> 00:20:54.120
where we would visit the
College Mental Health Program,

00:20:54.120 --> 00:20:56.820
CMHP for short, visits.

00:20:56.820 --> 00:21:01.820
So, MIT, McLean Host MIT,

00:21:02.070 --> 00:21:05.040
and then the CMHP,

00:21:05.040 --> 00:21:07.320
CMHP visit MIT and so forth.

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:09.720
And so just in doing that,

00:21:09.720 --> 00:21:13.440
like spending time in each
other's respective homes,

00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:15.000
that helped to build the trust.

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:17.650
And we would ask schools also

00:21:18.630 --> 00:21:21.660
when we'd learned the schools that we,

00:21:21.660 --> 00:21:24.300
we didn't know before
we started the program

00:21:24.300 --> 00:21:27.693
how many schools,

00:21:29.025 --> 00:21:31.110
the large number of schools
that we were seeing.

00:21:31.110 --> 00:21:33.060
So once we had that information,

00:21:33.060 --> 00:21:34.687
we reached out to those folks and said,

00:21:34.687 --> 00:21:38.160
"hey, do you know that
each year we're seeing

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:41.640
about 50 of your students in our setting,

00:21:41.640 --> 00:21:43.920
and would you be willing to get together

00:21:43.920 --> 00:21:46.900
and just talk about what's
working on your campus

00:21:47.880 --> 00:21:49.620
to address the needs of those students?

00:21:49.620 --> 00:21:54.030
And also, from your
perspective, how are we doing

00:21:54.030 --> 00:21:56.100
in addressing the needs of your students?"

00:21:56.100 --> 00:21:59.550
And just kind of opening
up, asking the question,

00:21:59.550 --> 00:22:02.490
often hearing great feedback,

00:22:02.490 --> 00:22:06.270
like, oh, we really love
working, but sometimes not.

00:22:06.270 --> 00:22:09.240
This is what isn't working
when our students come.

00:22:09.240 --> 00:22:13.440
And we've really worked hard
to elicit that honest feedback

00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:15.240
and said, just tell us,

00:22:15.240 --> 00:22:18.657
because this is all in
service of our students.

00:22:18.657 --> 00:22:22.710
And so without that information,
we can't get better.

00:22:22.710 --> 00:22:26.670
And we have information that
might be helpful for you

00:22:26.670 --> 00:22:30.570
in providing more effective
support of your students

00:22:30.570 --> 00:22:32.070
from a mental health perspective.

00:22:32.070 --> 00:22:34.740
And so really just building the trust in,

00:22:34.740 --> 00:22:36.780
that was just an example,
that programming,

00:22:36.780 --> 00:22:40.260
but we have kind of
dozens of those strategies

00:22:40.260 --> 00:22:41.250
for building the trust.

00:22:41.250 --> 00:22:43.500
But I just love that
you asked specifically

00:22:43.500 --> 00:22:45.480
about the term trust

00:22:45.480 --> 00:22:48.777
because that's absolutely what's required.

00:22:48.777 --> 00:22:51.810
And the main reason I know that is

00:22:51.810 --> 00:22:55.863
because I've been an
administrator on a college campus,

00:22:56.700 --> 00:23:00.880
and I know that colleges and universities

00:23:02.370 --> 00:23:04.980
can be really concerned and guarded

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:08.850
about what they're sharing
and who they welcome in

00:23:08.850 --> 00:23:11.827
to collaborate with them and so...

00:23:11.827 --> 00:23:13.333
- Yeah, and did you use that?
- Just build the trust.

00:23:13.333 --> 00:23:16.110
- In a sense, I mean,
did you mention the roles

00:23:16.110 --> 00:23:17.130
that you had had before

00:23:17.130 --> 00:23:17.963
as you're (indistinct)...
- Yes, yes.

00:23:17.963 --> 00:23:20.520
- Or did you just have let that, okay.

00:23:20.520 --> 00:23:21.353
- Actually did,

00:23:21.353 --> 00:23:23.841
and, especially, whenever
visiting the campuses

00:23:23.841 --> 00:23:28.841
because it, I think, just
lowers everyone's anxiety.

00:23:30.780 --> 00:23:34.140
And you can see this in any discipline

00:23:34.140 --> 00:23:36.150
if someone comes into your space

00:23:36.150 --> 00:23:39.900
and they've actually worked
in your type of setting.

00:23:39.900 --> 00:23:42.750
My job at the University of Michigan

00:23:42.750 --> 00:23:45.600
had oversight of college
student mental health.

00:23:45.600 --> 00:23:49.020
So when I'm engaging with
colleges and universities

00:23:49.020 --> 00:23:50.190
and they hear that,

00:23:50.190 --> 00:23:54.450
there's this almost like
an exhaling, like, okay,

00:23:54.450 --> 00:23:56.415
you get it.
- She gets it.

00:23:56.415 --> 00:23:57.248
- She gets it.

00:23:57.248 --> 00:23:59.490
We don't have to explain.
- Exactly.

00:23:59.490 --> 00:24:01.710
- 'Cause there's so many misconceptions

00:24:01.710 --> 00:24:06.710
about some of the challenges
that people are facing

00:24:06.720 --> 00:24:08.817
regarding college student mental health.

00:24:08.817 --> 00:24:10.530
And so there's that piece of it.

00:24:10.530 --> 00:24:12.600
The other thing that I did

00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:14.670
in my administrative role previously,

00:24:14.670 --> 00:24:17.490
when I worked at the
University of Michigan

00:24:17.490 --> 00:24:19.380
as Associate Dean, was also,

00:24:19.380 --> 00:24:22.530
I had oversight of critical
incident management

00:24:22.530 --> 00:24:25.740
and developing an approach and a structure

00:24:25.740 --> 00:24:28.650
and a strategy for anticipating

00:24:28.650 --> 00:24:32.070
and responding to crises on campus.

00:24:32.070 --> 00:24:36.480
It's another element of
campus life and college life

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:39.790
that people don't speak about very easily

00:24:41.820 --> 00:24:44.790
because a whole range of things can happen

00:24:44.790 --> 00:24:46.740
that can be extremely challenging.

00:24:46.740 --> 00:24:51.740
So I think those two things
combined really made,

00:24:53.400 --> 00:24:57.210
and continue to make it
easy for us to engage

00:24:57.210 --> 00:25:00.660
with campus professionals.

00:25:00.660 --> 00:25:01.950
- Yeah, I think that's really powerful,

00:25:01.950 --> 00:25:03.960
I mean, so much of the foundational work,

00:25:03.960 --> 00:25:06.150
and you spent the time doing that too.

00:25:06.150 --> 00:25:08.430
And I'm sure that that people sense that

00:25:08.430 --> 00:25:10.800
and knows that that time
and investment is there.

00:25:10.800 --> 00:25:12.180
And even as you were sharing just now not

00:25:12.180 --> 00:25:14.460
to pivot too far off, but I'm curious,

00:25:14.460 --> 00:25:16.590
as you talked about some
of the preventative things

00:25:16.590 --> 00:25:18.033
in anticipating crises,

00:25:19.350 --> 00:25:21.570
would you say that was a standard practice

00:25:21.570 --> 00:25:22.530
or was that something

00:25:22.530 --> 00:25:26.220
that was maybe more revolutionary sense?

00:25:26.220 --> 00:25:28.110
Just because from my limited scope,

00:25:28.110 --> 00:25:30.420
just seeing so many of our schools,

00:25:30.420 --> 00:25:31.620
and we've done this ourselves as well,

00:25:31.620 --> 00:25:34.590
they're reactive rather than proactive.

00:25:34.590 --> 00:25:36.030
So hearing you talk
about that from earlier,

00:25:36.030 --> 00:25:38.250
I'm curious if that was just something

00:25:38.250 --> 00:25:40.050
that you all were doing
that was ahead of the game

00:25:40.050 --> 00:25:41.850
in the sense or if you've
seen things change,

00:25:41.850 --> 00:25:43.860
are people more in that space?

00:25:43.860 --> 00:25:45.120
Because that seems like it has

00:25:45.120 --> 00:25:47.040
so many important implications for crises

00:25:47.040 --> 00:25:48.747
and also in terms of mental health as well

00:25:48.747 --> 00:25:50.910
and mental health crises that can come up.

00:25:50.910 --> 00:25:53.580
- That is a brilliant question

00:25:53.580 --> 00:25:56.283
and, again, right at
the heart of the matter.

00:25:57.840 --> 00:26:02.840
When I was recruited to
join the student life staff

00:26:04.620 --> 00:26:06.859
at the University of Michigan,

00:26:06.859 --> 00:26:10.230
what you just asked was
sort of the reason why,

00:26:10.230 --> 00:26:14.883
and this was now 20 years ago.

00:26:16.830 --> 00:26:20.400
And it was specifically
because this is often the case

00:26:20.400 --> 00:26:22.980
on many college campuses, they had,

00:26:22.980 --> 00:26:26.070
in their case, gone through a
particularly challenging year

00:26:26.070 --> 00:26:31.070
with a number of crises, a
full range of campus crises.

00:26:31.260 --> 00:26:35.160
And they had been doing
a really excellent job

00:26:35.160 --> 00:26:36.930
of responding to,

00:26:36.930 --> 00:26:40.500
but felt like they didn't
have an infrastructure

00:26:40.500 --> 00:26:42.720
for anticipating

00:26:42.720 --> 00:26:46.947
and also didn't have a strategic response,

00:26:46.947 --> 00:26:48.930
the things that were very clear,

00:26:48.930 --> 00:26:53.310
there were some phenomenal
people on the ground,

00:26:53.310 --> 00:26:57.033
like the vice president of
student affairs, for example,

00:26:58.020 --> 00:26:59.163
and deans.

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:03.900
And these are the people who
sort of had in their heads,

00:27:03.900 --> 00:27:06.750
what to do when a bad thing happens.

00:27:06.750 --> 00:27:08.820
And they were really, really good at it.

00:27:08.820 --> 00:27:12.510
And they would show up themselves
to wherever and whatever,

00:27:12.510 --> 00:27:14.520
any time of day or night.

00:27:14.520 --> 00:27:17.850
And what they experienced
at that particular year

00:27:17.850 --> 00:27:20.550
was that there was a
real spike in incidents

00:27:20.550 --> 00:27:22.800
and a broad range of them.

00:27:22.800 --> 00:27:24.930
And that they couldn't catch their breath,

00:27:24.930 --> 00:27:28.170
that they felt like they were
constantly putting out fires,

00:27:28.170 --> 00:27:31.050
this fire, onto the next
thing, onto the next thing.

00:27:31.050 --> 00:27:34.290
And that there was no time to really stop

00:27:34.290 --> 00:27:38.550
and think about really a
structure and a process.

00:27:38.550 --> 00:27:41.070
They had certain values and principles.

00:27:41.070 --> 00:27:43.593
The University of Michigan is a highly,

00:27:44.490 --> 00:27:47.250
highly oriented towards social justice.

00:27:47.250 --> 00:27:49.683
So an example would be that,

00:27:51.120 --> 00:27:54.090
an approach to responding to crises

00:27:54.090 --> 00:27:58.230
would always be, by definition,
multi-culturally informed.

00:27:58.230 --> 00:28:02.130
That's just steeped into the fabric of

00:28:02.130 --> 00:28:04.830
how the division of student life works

00:28:04.830 --> 00:28:07.830
and things at the organization.

00:28:07.830 --> 00:28:09.570
But how do you create that

00:28:09.570 --> 00:28:13.800
so that people can come into
the system and understand

00:28:13.800 --> 00:28:17.643
what that looks like and
document it and teach people.

00:28:18.720 --> 00:28:22.710
And then create an
opportunity for the university

00:28:22.710 --> 00:28:27.660
to be more proactive
in anticipating crises

00:28:27.660 --> 00:28:30.990
so that we're out of that reactive mode,

00:28:30.990 --> 00:28:32.490
and able, not always,

00:28:32.490 --> 00:28:35.010
because there will always be emergencies,

00:28:35.010 --> 00:28:39.360
but in some instances, you can
look six months down the road

00:28:39.360 --> 00:28:41.790
and say, "oh, this controversial speaker

00:28:41.790 --> 00:28:43.590
is coming to campus.

00:28:43.590 --> 00:28:45.630
What are the things we can do now

00:28:45.630 --> 00:28:49.230
to prepare our institution

00:28:49.230 --> 00:28:52.500
to respond to what is going to be

00:28:52.500 --> 00:28:55.620
a really challenging
situation more effectively?"

00:28:55.620 --> 00:28:58.530
And you can do that for
a whole range of crises

00:28:58.530 --> 00:29:01.140
that inevitably are going to happen

00:29:01.140 --> 00:29:02.520
at one point or another.

00:29:02.520 --> 00:29:05.430
So it really was about
creating an infrastructure

00:29:05.430 --> 00:29:08.910
so people could get out
of that reactive mode

00:29:08.910 --> 00:29:13.320
and feel more and be more
proactive in anticipating

00:29:13.320 --> 00:29:15.930
and more thoughtful in responding to.

00:29:15.930 --> 00:29:17.760
We had a whole range of,

00:29:17.760 --> 00:29:22.230
anything could end up being
a crisis on a college campus.

00:29:22.230 --> 00:29:25.860
One that sticks in my mind at the time

00:29:25.860 --> 00:29:29.190
was the Asian tsunami that
happened at that time.

00:29:29.190 --> 00:29:32.400
And you're talking about
a large university system

00:29:32.400 --> 00:29:37.400
with a significant international
student population.

00:29:37.500 --> 00:29:41.580
That tsunami happened
during semester break

00:29:41.580 --> 00:29:44.640
so many of our students were not on campus

00:29:44.640 --> 00:29:47.130
but in their respective
parts of the world.

00:29:47.130 --> 00:29:48.573
So just think about that.

00:29:50.460 --> 00:29:51.900
How do you respond in a way

00:29:51.900 --> 00:29:56.900
that's gonna also be
multi-culturally responsive as well?

00:29:57.810 --> 00:30:01.050
So I think that was all long
answer to your question,

00:30:01.050 --> 00:30:02.220
but I... (laughs)

00:30:02.220 --> 00:30:03.083
- Yeah, I mean, so powerful.

00:30:03.083 --> 00:30:05.910
I mean, so informed by all
the different things...

00:30:05.910 --> 00:30:07.710
- And from a mental, I'm sorry,

00:30:07.710 --> 00:30:09.420
from a mental health perspective,

00:30:09.420 --> 00:30:11.310
we designed new strategies

00:30:11.310 --> 00:30:15.450
that now have become really
standards of best practice,

00:30:15.450 --> 00:30:19.080
like creating a critical
incident response team,

00:30:19.080 --> 00:30:22.650
or what many schools
refer to as a behavior,

00:30:22.650 --> 00:30:24.840
there are all these
different names for it.

00:30:24.840 --> 00:30:28.230
But specifically at the
intersection of critical incidents

00:30:28.230 --> 00:30:29.610
and mental health,

00:30:29.610 --> 00:30:33.120
a lot of the structures
that we take for granted now

00:30:33.120 --> 00:30:34.770
on college campuses,

00:30:34.770 --> 00:30:38.130
those kinds of teams
and that team approach,

00:30:38.130 --> 00:30:40.680
we started those at the
University of Michigan.

00:30:40.680 --> 00:30:42.630
- Wow, that's really encouraging to hear

00:30:42.630 --> 00:30:44.730
and to know the foundation of it as well,

00:30:44.730 --> 00:30:46.470
and the ways that you all
were so forward thinking.

00:30:46.470 --> 00:30:48.180
I mean, even as you're sharing now,

00:30:48.180 --> 00:30:51.150
still so many things that
we can learn from that.

00:30:51.150 --> 00:30:53.370
So, again, just encouraged
and not surprised

00:30:53.370 --> 00:30:55.320
to know that you had your hand then,

00:30:55.320 --> 00:30:56.310
just the team there,

00:30:56.310 --> 00:30:59.820
it seems like was really
intentional about that as well.

00:30:59.820 --> 00:31:00.990
- Yeah.

00:31:00.990 --> 00:31:03.120
- In lot of ways, it also
makes me think about the book

00:31:03.120 --> 00:31:06.060
that you just published, I
guess, about a year ago as well,

00:31:06.060 --> 00:31:07.080
because in some ways,

00:31:07.080 --> 00:31:09.180
it seems like it has multiple components,

00:31:09.180 --> 00:31:11.160
both the preventative piece,

00:31:11.160 --> 00:31:13.560
but then also it's informed
by so many vignettes

00:31:13.560 --> 00:31:15.300
and stories.

00:31:15.300 --> 00:31:16.860
So not that it's a reaction to,

00:31:16.860 --> 00:31:18.720
but in some ways it also
feels like a response

00:31:18.720 --> 00:31:21.810
to things that have been
happening for quite a while,

00:31:21.810 --> 00:31:25.530
even as you're trying to help
create these more diverse

00:31:25.530 --> 00:31:28.390
and inclusive cultures and environments

00:31:29.310 --> 00:31:31.410
without doing things
that will be temporary

00:31:31.410 --> 00:31:35.400
or just tokenized or all
those other many other types

00:31:35.400 --> 00:31:36.733
of words we could throw in there.

00:31:36.733 --> 00:31:38.280
I was curious if you could
just share a little bit

00:31:38.280 --> 00:31:41.823
about the impetus for writing
the book in the place.

00:31:43.140 --> 00:31:45.600
- That's right, you just
picked up on something else.

00:31:45.600 --> 00:31:47.534
It's so important for this work.

00:31:47.534 --> 00:31:52.200
And it's something that I carry
with me in my work at McLean

00:31:52.200 --> 00:31:53.670
in the College Mental Health Program

00:31:53.670 --> 00:31:56.190
and in the Diversity
Equity Inclusion Office,

00:31:56.190 --> 00:31:59.040
and specifically in
consulting the colleges

00:31:59.040 --> 00:32:03.827
and universities, when we engage
in partnership with schools

00:32:05.130 --> 00:32:08.760
or any organization to
sort of do the work,

00:32:08.760 --> 00:32:12.543
address sort of a crisis
or a critical incident,

00:32:13.710 --> 00:32:15.960
what we ask, and this I really learned

00:32:15.960 --> 00:32:18.930
from having been in the critical incident,

00:32:18.930 --> 00:32:22.560
what we ask is, in our contract,

00:32:22.560 --> 00:32:25.530
let's figure out together
what's gonna be sustainable,

00:32:25.530 --> 00:32:28.053
which is one of the themes of the book.

00:32:29.587 --> 00:32:31.380
We're not gonna do a one and done.

00:32:31.380 --> 00:32:32.880
One and done doesn't work.

00:32:32.880 --> 00:32:35.820
So we won't partner with an organization.

00:32:35.820 --> 00:32:39.150
We don't want to
perpetuate that tokenizing,

00:32:39.150 --> 00:32:42.360
oh, we're throwing
resources at this thing,

00:32:42.360 --> 00:32:47.310
we're focused on this issue,
this crisis for this semester.

00:32:47.310 --> 00:32:48.300
And then guess what?

00:32:48.300 --> 00:32:51.030
Next semester we're onto the next crisis.

00:32:51.030 --> 00:32:52.467
We're not doing that.

00:32:52.467 --> 00:32:54.300
And we know how that happens.

00:32:54.300 --> 00:32:58.290
So let's figure out together
how, from the outset,

00:32:58.290 --> 00:33:02.010
what we put in place can
be sustained over time.

00:33:02.010 --> 00:33:04.800
And we'll work in partnership
to figure that out

00:33:04.800 --> 00:33:06.780
because it serves no one,

00:33:06.780 --> 00:33:08.880
least of all, your students

00:33:08.880 --> 00:33:12.420
to bring this intervention one year

00:33:12.420 --> 00:33:15.900
and have it disappear the next.

00:33:15.900 --> 00:33:20.280
So, yeah, so really a
through line, if you will.

00:33:20.280 --> 00:33:23.520
And your question about the book,

00:33:23.520 --> 00:33:26.553
the impetus you asked
about for writing the book.

00:33:29.190 --> 00:33:30.213
So many things.

00:33:33.300 --> 00:33:36.570
As I think about for Dr.
Lauren Wadsworth and myself,

00:33:36.570 --> 00:33:38.190
there were a couple of things.

00:33:38.190 --> 00:33:43.190
One, we both have hold
minoritized identities

00:33:44.070 --> 00:33:47.760
within academic and medical settings.

00:33:47.760 --> 00:33:51.580
And we had our individual
and shared experiences

00:33:52.470 --> 00:33:55.050
that sort of came together

00:33:55.050 --> 00:33:57.150
while working at a
particular organization.

00:33:57.150 --> 00:33:59.230
And we were being called upon

00:34:00.120 --> 00:34:04.983
to serve as experts on
different areas that,

00:34:05.850 --> 00:34:08.550
my expertise, for
example, within my system,

00:34:08.550 --> 00:34:11.940
I was recruited there specifically
because of my expertise

00:34:11.940 --> 00:34:13.950
in college student mental health

00:34:13.950 --> 00:34:15.930
and critical incident management.

00:34:15.930 --> 00:34:19.170
Now, it just so happens that,

00:34:19.170 --> 00:34:22.650
I think that culturally
responsive treatment

00:34:22.650 --> 00:34:25.740
should be integral to
all treatment approaches,

00:34:25.740 --> 00:34:29.133
to all psychology programs,

00:34:30.660 --> 00:34:32.850
but that's not always the case.

00:34:32.850 --> 00:34:36.690
So Lauren and I had
some shared experiences

00:34:36.690 --> 00:34:40.650
and sort of being
continuously sort of look to

00:34:40.650 --> 00:34:43.500
as the expert in all things,

00:34:43.500 --> 00:34:45.390
diversity, equity, inclusion related.

00:34:45.390 --> 00:34:49.680
And at a certain point we had
developed so many workshops

00:34:49.680 --> 00:34:52.890
and talks and we just decided together

00:34:52.890 --> 00:34:55.380
why don't we just document
some of these things.

00:34:55.380 --> 00:34:57.540
So put some of this in writing,

00:34:57.540 --> 00:34:59.667
as well as some of these experiences.

00:34:59.667 --> 00:35:02.763
And that was sort of one driving force.

00:35:03.868 --> 00:35:05.280
So sort of to to be more efficient.

00:35:05.280 --> 00:35:08.610
So we've said this thing so many times,

00:35:08.610 --> 00:35:10.710
how about we commit it to paper?

00:35:10.710 --> 00:35:12.570
We don't have to repeat it all again.

00:35:12.570 --> 00:35:17.570
But there were couple of other
driving forces, and one was,

00:35:19.320 --> 00:35:24.240
we were really obsessed with
this idea of inclusivity

00:35:24.240 --> 00:35:28.860
and helping organizations to
think about how to move beyond

00:35:28.860 --> 00:35:32.700
what I refer to as
diversity by the numbers

00:35:32.700 --> 00:35:35.280
to actually thinking about

00:35:35.280 --> 00:35:37.980
what does an inclusive
workplace look like,

00:35:37.980 --> 00:35:39.840
what are those practices,

00:35:39.840 --> 00:35:42.120
what are those behaviors and those skills

00:35:42.120 --> 00:35:47.120
so that as we are so highly
focused and motivated,

00:35:47.130 --> 00:35:49.380
especially in recent years,

00:35:49.380 --> 00:35:54.380
to recruit diverse talent
into our organizations.

00:35:55.200 --> 00:35:57.030
What can we do to ensure

00:35:57.030 --> 00:35:59.610
that we are prepared to really receive

00:35:59.610 --> 00:36:02.940
and welcome diverse talent
to ensure that folks,

00:36:02.940 --> 00:36:06.450
when they show up in our organizations,

00:36:06.450 --> 00:36:10.350
that people feel safe or relatively safe,

00:36:10.350 --> 00:36:15.350
that they feel valued, that
they feel heard and seen?

00:36:16.950 --> 00:36:18.810
So kind of really breaking it down

00:36:18.810 --> 00:36:22.350
into what does the practice
of inclusivity look like.

00:36:22.350 --> 00:36:25.830
So that piece of it, which
is kind of a lot of people,

00:36:25.830 --> 00:36:30.030
I mean, some really great
thinkers in that space, of course.

00:36:30.030 --> 00:36:31.740
And then the other piece

00:36:31.740 --> 00:36:34.620
that really drove the
structure of the book was,

00:36:34.620 --> 00:36:36.720
how do we make this plane?

00:36:36.720 --> 00:36:39.300
We both happened to be
clinical psychologists.

00:36:39.300 --> 00:36:43.530
We're also researchers and clinicians.

00:36:43.530 --> 00:36:45.630
We wanted to bring what we knew

00:36:45.630 --> 00:36:47.730
from the practice of psychology,

00:36:47.730 --> 00:36:51.150
like evidence-based
strategies, to the work,

00:36:51.150 --> 00:36:53.760
but lose the jargon.

00:36:53.760 --> 00:36:57.120
So keep the science, but lose the jargon.

00:36:57.120 --> 00:36:58.770
- That's an impressive skill.

00:36:58.770 --> 00:37:00.063
That's so important.
(both laughs)

00:37:00.063 --> 00:37:01.710
- It's so important, right?

00:37:01.710 --> 00:37:03.360
And it's a lifelong skill.

00:37:03.360 --> 00:37:05.820
I'm fascinated by that.

00:37:05.820 --> 00:37:07.590
But we wanted to just make it plain

00:37:07.590 --> 00:37:11.910
so that folks didn't have to
peel through layers and layers

00:37:11.910 --> 00:37:13.920
of, I mean, the data are there

00:37:13.920 --> 00:37:15.510
and the statistics and everything,

00:37:15.510 --> 00:37:18.660
but really not have to
look up every other word

00:37:18.660 --> 00:37:21.060
to figure out what the heck
are they talking about.

00:37:21.060 --> 00:37:24.120
We wanted to make it plain so
that people could pick it up

00:37:24.120 --> 00:37:27.150
and find the skills accessible

00:37:27.150 --> 00:37:30.090
and like, oh, I could do that thing.

00:37:30.090 --> 00:37:32.160
I could do that today.

00:37:32.160 --> 00:37:35.520
And I don't have to be
an expert in X, Y, and Z

00:37:35.520 --> 00:37:38.220
in order to do that and
to practice that skill.

00:37:38.220 --> 00:37:42.570
So sort of those things
combined really shaped the book

00:37:42.570 --> 00:37:47.570
and specifically, the structure
and the content of the book.

00:37:47.610 --> 00:37:52.610
It's why we chose to begin with
vignettes real life stories

00:37:54.570 --> 00:37:55.890
to make it plain,

00:37:55.890 --> 00:38:00.890
so that people could just read
or hear a story and think,

00:38:01.177 --> 00:38:03.570
"oh, my goodness, I've seen that before,

00:38:03.570 --> 00:38:05.280
or I've experienced that,

00:38:05.280 --> 00:38:06.943
or I've said that."

00:38:06.943 --> 00:38:10.110
I can't tell you how much
feedback we've gotten

00:38:10.110 --> 00:38:14.610
from folks saying "I've seen
myself in those vignettes

00:38:14.610 --> 00:38:17.640
and not always on the receiving end

00:38:17.640 --> 00:38:20.070
of identity related aggressions,

00:38:20.070 --> 00:38:22.827
but sometimes as the perpetrator."

00:38:23.730 --> 00:38:25.830
So connecting with the stories

00:38:25.830 --> 00:38:29.190
and then that's why there's
the vignette opening,

00:38:29.190 --> 00:38:32.040
and then the next part of the structure

00:38:32.040 --> 00:38:35.190
is kind of like, what's
wrong with this picture?

00:38:35.190 --> 00:38:36.570
Why is that programmatic?

00:38:36.570 --> 00:38:40.260
So just making it really
plain, this is not okay.

00:38:40.260 --> 00:38:41.310
Here's why.

00:38:41.310 --> 00:38:43.740
If you don't already know, here's why.

00:38:43.740 --> 00:38:44.640
Lots of people don't know.

00:38:44.640 --> 00:38:47.160
And then what to do about it?

00:38:47.160 --> 00:38:49.320
Which is a question that I think people

00:38:49.320 --> 00:38:52.140
have been really sort of pressed to answer

00:38:52.140 --> 00:38:53.460
in the past few years.

00:38:53.460 --> 00:38:56.670
Yes, in this time of racial
reckoning and unrest,

00:38:56.670 --> 00:38:57.660
what do we do?

00:38:57.660 --> 00:38:59.700
What do we actually need to do?

00:38:59.700 --> 00:39:04.560
And so really practical,
skills-based, evidence-driven,

00:39:04.560 --> 00:39:06.780
but like I said, without the jargon.

00:39:06.780 --> 00:39:08.970
- Yeah, that's really
powerful on so many levels.

00:39:08.970 --> 00:39:11.040
And even that comment that you mentioned

00:39:11.040 --> 00:39:12.720
about some of the feedback
that you're getting.

00:39:12.720 --> 00:39:14.370
People even realizing

00:39:14.370 --> 00:39:17.850
that they've maybe sometimes
perpetuate things unknowingly.

00:39:17.850 --> 00:39:19.200
I mean, two things that stand out there.

00:39:19.200 --> 00:39:21.060
One, the fact that people
were reading the book

00:39:21.060 --> 00:39:22.470
in the first place,

00:39:22.470 --> 00:39:24.180
which makes me think they were open to it,

00:39:24.180 --> 00:39:26.250
but then also that may
have given them a chance

00:39:26.250 --> 00:39:29.460
to see things in the way they
may not have caught before

00:39:29.460 --> 00:39:31.440
and then to actually have
practical things they can do

00:39:31.440 --> 00:39:32.280
to walk that out.

00:39:32.280 --> 00:39:35.220
So, I mean, I think that's so impactful

00:39:35.220 --> 00:39:36.510
in so many different ways.

00:39:36.510 --> 00:39:38.220
And there's one of the
things I was curious about.

00:39:38.220 --> 00:39:40.800
If people are actually seeing
best practices come out

00:39:40.800 --> 00:39:42.000
of their reading of the book,

00:39:42.000 --> 00:39:44.310
and it sounds like you're
definitely getting that feedback.

00:39:44.310 --> 00:39:46.740
And I imagine that can have
so many ripple effects,

00:39:46.740 --> 00:39:48.180
even as you talked about
the theme of the book,

00:39:48.180 --> 00:39:50.160
trying to have these sustainable things

00:39:50.160 --> 00:39:52.020
that really change cultures,

00:39:52.020 --> 00:39:55.410
which is no small task by any means.

00:39:55.410 --> 00:39:57.063
So just encouraging to
hear that type of feedback

00:39:57.063 --> 00:39:58.770
that you've gotten already.

00:39:58.770 --> 00:40:00.060
- Thank you.

00:40:00.060 --> 00:40:05.060
We are both humbled and
encouraged by the feedback,

00:40:06.362 --> 00:40:10.440
and we're learning more every day

00:40:10.440 --> 00:40:15.000
just from how organizations
are utilizing the book,

00:40:15.000 --> 00:40:16.290
responding to the book.

00:40:16.290 --> 00:40:19.890
One of the things we had
hoped for in writing the book

00:40:19.890 --> 00:40:24.873
was that the work could be
accessible across industries.

00:40:26.340 --> 00:40:27.423
But we had no idea.

00:40:28.560 --> 00:40:30.750
Again, hit the nail on
the head when you said,

00:40:30.750 --> 00:40:32.190
well, I'm happy to hear that people are,

00:40:32.190 --> 00:40:34.890
A, reading the book, 'cause
we certainly did know like,

00:40:34.890 --> 00:40:36.270
is anyone gonna read this?

00:40:36.270 --> 00:40:37.103
You don't know.

00:40:37.103 --> 00:40:39.300
You just put this thing
out there in the world

00:40:39.300 --> 00:40:44.300
and pray and hope that
someone will resonate with it.

00:40:44.490 --> 00:40:48.060
So we are really encouraged

00:40:48.060 --> 00:40:52.020
and humbled by the range of industries

00:40:52.020 --> 00:40:54.090
that have been selecting the book

00:40:54.090 --> 00:40:56.880
as their organization reed.

00:40:56.880 --> 00:40:58.563
And we're seeing that in,

00:40:59.400 --> 00:41:02.910
not surprisingly in academic settings

00:41:02.910 --> 00:41:04.193
and in institutions of higher education.

00:41:04.193 --> 00:41:06.420
I mean, that's kind of where we lived.

00:41:06.420 --> 00:41:07.830
Not that we expected that.

00:41:07.830 --> 00:41:10.410
We hoped that for that.

00:41:10.410 --> 00:41:13.830
But it's being selected
by secondary schools

00:41:13.830 --> 00:41:17.793
and that's really encouraging by finance,

00:41:18.810 --> 00:41:22.140
in financial spaces, law firms,

00:41:22.140 --> 00:41:26.640
the American Society
for Clinical Oncology.

00:41:26.640 --> 00:41:28.650
We've been going on the book tour,

00:41:28.650 --> 00:41:30.510
much of which has been virtual,

00:41:30.510 --> 00:41:34.020
but more recently, now it's in person.

00:41:34.020 --> 00:41:36.240
And so we are learning so much

00:41:36.240 --> 00:41:38.760
because these types of organizations,

00:41:38.760 --> 00:41:42.993
again, across industries,
theater, publishing,

00:41:44.670 --> 00:41:47.520
are selecting the book
as their community reads

00:41:47.520 --> 00:41:51.600
and then inviting us into
this space, research labs,

00:41:51.600 --> 00:41:54.423
the Tsai Center at Yale was one.

00:41:55.680 --> 00:41:57.840
But it's just such a different experience

00:41:57.840 --> 00:42:00.720
to go into the space
where the entire workforce

00:42:00.720 --> 00:42:03.150
has been working with the book

00:42:03.150 --> 00:42:06.120
and then they have a range
of questions and issues

00:42:06.120 --> 00:42:07.113
and responses.

00:42:10.050 --> 00:42:13.660
It's been just so encouraging to see

00:42:14.520 --> 00:42:16.740
how people are leaning into the work

00:42:16.740 --> 00:42:21.740
and really eager to take
steps to learn the practices.

00:42:21.900 --> 00:42:24.090
So you asked about best practices,

00:42:24.090 --> 00:42:29.090
I feel like best practices
are emerging all of the time.

00:42:29.400 --> 00:42:32.100
From our perspective,
through this process,

00:42:32.100 --> 00:42:33.330
we're learning so much.

00:42:33.330 --> 00:42:38.330
We have a consulting company
and we also have workshops

00:42:38.460 --> 00:42:40.860
that are aligned with
the chapters in the book.

00:42:40.860 --> 00:42:44.700
So sometimes, not always, but
organizations will invite us

00:42:44.700 --> 00:42:46.890
to do a book signing and a book talk,

00:42:46.890 --> 00:42:51.890
but then also to deliver
workshops and the top,

00:42:52.950 --> 00:42:54.780
there are several, but the number,

00:42:54.780 --> 00:42:59.160
well, the top two requests
are to learn how to do

00:42:59.160 --> 00:43:00.510
what we write about in the book

00:43:00.510 --> 00:43:03.570
as empowered versus oppressive listening.

00:43:03.570 --> 00:43:06.840
So that's emerging as a best practice.

00:43:06.840 --> 00:43:11.840
And then the other is, not
surprisingly, how to recognize,

00:43:12.210 --> 00:43:14.160
respond to, and reduce the rate

00:43:14.160 --> 00:43:16.140
of identity related aggressions,

00:43:16.140 --> 00:43:20.220
which is the term that we use
to refer to microaggressions.

00:43:20.220 --> 00:43:24.960
So, primarily, it
switches as time evolves,

00:43:24.960 --> 00:43:27.030
but right now those are the areas

00:43:27.030 --> 00:43:28.710
we are getting the most request

00:43:28.710 --> 00:43:31.410
to come and help us think how to practice

00:43:31.410 --> 00:43:32.880
and learn these skills.

00:43:32.880 --> 00:43:34.770
- Yeah, that does sound so encouraging.

00:43:34.770 --> 00:43:36.630
And I'm curious, for our listeners too,

00:43:36.630 --> 00:43:38.040
especially those who might not have heard

00:43:38.040 --> 00:43:39.720
the identity related aggressions as well,

00:43:39.720 --> 00:43:41.850
can you elaborate a little bit on that

00:43:41.850 --> 00:43:44.490
and why that term and not microaggressions

00:43:44.490 --> 00:43:46.770
and where it has shifted
in terms of your thinking

00:43:46.770 --> 00:43:49.230
and what you've seen from others as well?

00:43:49.230 --> 00:43:53.610
- Yeah, and we're hearing lots about,

00:43:53.610 --> 00:43:56.580
what people's language is
I think really important.

00:43:56.580 --> 00:44:00.000
And so the term microaggressions,
there's been some,

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:02.550
that refers to sort of everyday slight,

00:44:02.550 --> 00:44:04.580
often, unintentional...

00:44:07.080 --> 00:44:10.740
sort of targeting some aspect

00:44:10.740 --> 00:44:12.840
of an individual's
social/cultural identity.

00:44:12.840 --> 00:44:16.890
So maybe race, ethnicity,
gender, sexual orientation,

00:44:16.890 --> 00:44:18.480
age, the whole range,

00:44:18.480 --> 00:44:23.480
but unintended everyday slights
that create harm over time.

00:44:24.540 --> 00:44:29.540
And so that term that's commonly
known as microaggressions.

00:44:30.390 --> 00:44:32.130
And there's been a discussion,

00:44:32.130 --> 00:44:36.540
a lot of discourse over recent
years about the term micro,

00:44:36.540 --> 00:44:39.750
which actually means
several different things

00:44:39.750 --> 00:44:41.730
when you look at the
people who coin the term,

00:44:41.730 --> 00:44:46.440
but some people were sort of pushing back

00:44:46.440 --> 00:44:48.000
against the micro part,

00:44:48.000 --> 00:44:52.620
feeling or believing that
it minimizes the damage.

00:44:52.620 --> 00:44:56.730
And as we were writing the
book, we were thinking that,

00:44:56.730 --> 00:45:00.690
when the term microaggression
was first introduced,

00:45:00.690 --> 00:45:05.400
that it was probably necessary
to use a term like that

00:45:05.400 --> 00:45:09.177
to help people understand the harm

00:45:11.880 --> 00:45:16.880
and not feel so guilty about it

00:45:17.130 --> 00:45:19.620
that they couldn't even delve into

00:45:19.620 --> 00:45:21.450
why this is so problematic,

00:45:21.450 --> 00:45:24.210
but we feel like we're
far enough in the field

00:45:24.210 --> 00:45:28.800
that we wanted to respond
and eliminate the micro piece

00:45:28.800 --> 00:45:33.800
and really focus instead on the impact,

00:45:34.500 --> 00:45:38.820
centering the identities
of individuals frequently

00:45:38.820 --> 00:45:41.160
with marginalized and
minoritized identities

00:45:41.160 --> 00:45:46.160
who are on the receiving end
of these everyday slights

00:45:47.130 --> 00:45:48.330
and insult.

00:45:48.330 --> 00:45:51.330
And so we've took out the micro

00:45:51.330 --> 00:45:54.720
and replaced it with
identity related aggressions

00:45:54.720 --> 00:45:56.700
to do two things,

00:45:56.700 --> 00:45:58.920
sort of remove that piece about,

00:45:58.920 --> 00:46:01.050
make sure there's no misconception,

00:46:01.050 --> 00:46:06.000
the impact of these
behaviors is significant.

00:46:06.000 --> 00:46:09.780
It is major, it's really
hurtful and harmful to people.

00:46:09.780 --> 00:46:13.920
So coin the term, I coined the term IRAs,

00:46:13.920 --> 00:46:15.300
Identity Related Aggressions,

00:46:15.300 --> 00:46:19.230
also sort of picking up
on the financial term,

00:46:19.230 --> 00:46:21.690
helping people to remember that the impact

00:46:21.690 --> 00:46:24.570
isn't in the individual insult,

00:46:24.570 --> 00:46:29.550
but it is in the accumulation
of these everyday slights,

00:46:29.550 --> 00:46:33.060
but it accumulates over
time and the physical

00:46:33.060 --> 00:46:36.930
and the psychological impact
on people really takes a toll,

00:46:36.930 --> 00:46:39.450
well documented toll scientifically,

00:46:39.450 --> 00:46:42.093
and we really wanted to
highlight that aspect of it.

00:46:43.440 --> 00:46:44.850
- Yeah, I think that's so helpful.

00:46:44.850 --> 00:46:47.070
Yeah, and just really, as you mentioned,

00:46:47.070 --> 00:46:47.997
things have shifted in time.

00:46:47.997 --> 00:46:51.120
And just where we are, it
sounds very appropriate to me

00:46:51.120 --> 00:46:53.370
and really just helps
center, as you mentioned too.

00:46:53.370 --> 00:46:55.380
So I'm hopeful that you've
probably gotten this feedback

00:46:55.380 --> 00:46:58.800
that's really helping people
grasp it in a different way,

00:46:58.800 --> 00:46:59.940
and in a new way as well.

00:46:59.940 --> 00:47:02.640
'Cause even hearing high
school students talk

00:47:02.640 --> 00:47:03.473
about some of these things,

00:47:03.473 --> 00:47:05.310
those conversations
have definitely come up

00:47:05.310 --> 00:47:07.590
in terms of the aspects
with the word micro.

00:47:07.590 --> 00:47:09.930
So, again, not surprised
that you're at the forefront

00:47:09.930 --> 00:47:11.550
and thinking ahead for many of us

00:47:11.550 --> 00:47:13.503
and helping us really frame these

00:47:13.503 --> 00:47:14.820
in a really appropriate way.

00:47:14.820 --> 00:47:16.290
So definitely appreciate that as well.

00:47:16.290 --> 00:47:18.540
- I don't know that I'm at the forefront,

00:47:18.540 --> 00:47:23.160
but just definitely on
the journey together.

00:47:23.160 --> 00:47:24.810
It's just a shared journey.

00:47:24.810 --> 00:47:27.870
So we're all learning from each other.

00:47:27.870 --> 00:47:30.590
And we've been learning so much from...

00:47:34.154 --> 00:47:35.880
I'm thinking, again,
about a best practice,

00:47:35.880 --> 00:47:39.520
one best practice that's been
emerging that Lauren and I

00:47:40.440 --> 00:47:42.300
are learning a lot through this year

00:47:42.300 --> 00:47:45.810
of having been on the book
tour is how important it is

00:47:45.810 --> 00:47:48.930
in organizations, companies, groups

00:47:48.930 --> 00:47:52.830
to give people permission to name the ism,

00:47:52.830 --> 00:47:56.640
refer to, call a thing a thing.

00:47:56.640 --> 00:47:58.680
And because historically,

00:47:58.680 --> 00:48:01.770
we're really taught not to say these words

00:48:01.770 --> 00:48:04.770
and not to be able to talk about racism

00:48:04.770 --> 00:48:09.693
and sexism and transphobia
and ageism and ableism,

00:48:10.890 --> 00:48:12.960
really taught not to say those things.

00:48:12.960 --> 00:48:16.800
We're more comfortable, at
this point, naming sexism

00:48:16.800 --> 00:48:18.630
I think as a society,

00:48:18.630 --> 00:48:21.810
but stuck almost to the point of paralysis

00:48:21.810 --> 00:48:24.150
when it comes to naming racism.

00:48:24.150 --> 00:48:26.700
And that, in itself,
I think is interesting

00:48:26.700 --> 00:48:28.380
and helpful to think about.

00:48:28.380 --> 00:48:30.810
But we're seeing how important it is

00:48:30.810 --> 00:48:33.300
to give folks permission to name the ism

00:48:33.300 --> 00:48:36.450
and also to practice,

00:48:36.450 --> 00:48:38.130
practice, practice, practice.

00:48:38.130 --> 00:48:40.260
You've gotta practice these skills.

00:48:40.260 --> 00:48:44.190
So we really are very
intentional about creating spaces

00:48:44.190 --> 00:48:45.960
and opportunities for folks

00:48:45.960 --> 00:48:49.320
to hear themselves saying the words

00:48:49.320 --> 00:48:52.350
and hear each other saying the words.

00:48:52.350 --> 00:48:55.470
And it's just like, wow, I said that.

00:48:55.470 --> 00:48:58.410
I said that thing just happened.

00:48:58.410 --> 00:48:59.370
It's not okay.

00:48:59.370 --> 00:49:01.950
It sounds like it really
comes from a place

00:49:01.950 --> 00:49:04.170
of historical racism.

00:49:04.170 --> 00:49:07.860
It's not okay, it's not who we want to be.

00:49:07.860 --> 00:49:11.553
So let's figure out how we
address it moving forward.

00:49:12.607 --> 00:49:15.870
- So I feel like that's been
an emerging best practice.

00:49:15.870 --> 00:49:17.960
I can go on if you want here.

00:49:17.960 --> 00:49:18.793
(Stephanie laughs)
- These are great.

00:49:18.793 --> 00:49:19.626
These are great.

00:49:20.550 --> 00:49:23.490
Well, my curiosity is also peeking as well

00:49:23.490 --> 00:49:25.260
because we touched on at the
beginning of the episode,

00:49:25.260 --> 00:49:28.320
just having you share a little
bit about just your own,

00:49:28.320 --> 00:49:30.630
I used the word balance loosely, juggling,

00:49:30.630 --> 00:49:33.180
because as I'm hearing all the impressive

00:49:33.180 --> 00:49:34.950
and important work that you're doing,

00:49:34.950 --> 00:49:37.950
I'm just curious how you have
that cadence for yourself,

00:49:37.950 --> 00:49:40.410
as you even talked about
you and your partner

00:49:40.410 --> 00:49:42.660
just making sure that
you have time to pause.

00:49:42.660 --> 00:49:45.810
And how are you in that
aspect of the journey?

00:49:45.810 --> 00:49:47.220
As much as you're willing to share.

00:49:47.220 --> 00:49:49.110
And, again, this is
partially a selfish question

00:49:49.110 --> 00:49:51.360
because I get that question about myself.

00:49:51.360 --> 00:49:54.870
And I'm always thinking about
with the many different roles

00:49:54.870 --> 00:49:56.730
and initiatives and
responsibilities I have.

00:49:56.730 --> 00:50:00.180
So I will divulge that there
is some personal investment

00:50:00.180 --> 00:50:01.916
in the question as well.

00:50:01.916 --> 00:50:04.443
Curious about that regardlessly.

00:50:04.443 --> 00:50:09.060
- So, full disclosure,
I mean, this idea of,

00:50:09.060 --> 00:50:10.740
I talked about that I'm really obsessed

00:50:10.740 --> 00:50:12.230
with this notion of...

00:50:15.120 --> 00:50:18.390
sort of operationalizing
inclusivity and belonging,

00:50:18.390 --> 00:50:23.070
but just as important is this
notion of sustainability.

00:50:23.070 --> 00:50:26.703
And so, as we were talking about earlier,

00:50:27.540 --> 00:50:31.770
putting forth an approach or
a practice, an intervention,

00:50:31.770 --> 00:50:34.230
but ensuring that the
thing that you're doing,

00:50:34.230 --> 00:50:37.830
whatever that is, can be
there for the long haul.

00:50:37.830 --> 00:50:40.920
And that's been definitely a through line.

00:50:40.920 --> 00:50:44.670
It's a question that I think about

00:50:44.670 --> 00:50:48.060
since becoming diversity
equity inclusion officer.

00:50:48.060 --> 00:50:52.800
It's really a guiding approach
for how we do the work.

00:50:52.800 --> 00:50:56.010
In fact, even our core staff,

00:50:56.010 --> 00:50:59.190
as we're thinking about a solution

00:50:59.190 --> 00:51:03.000
or a response to something or new program,

00:51:03.000 --> 00:51:06.600
we always routinely ask ourselves,

00:51:06.600 --> 00:51:08.630
is this thing sustainable...

00:51:09.900 --> 00:51:13.770
for ourselves and for the organization?

00:51:13.770 --> 00:51:17.370
And if it's not, if we
can't introduce it today

00:51:17.370 --> 00:51:21.030
and figure out how it's
gonna still be a thing

00:51:21.030 --> 00:51:23.580
in six months in six years,

00:51:23.580 --> 00:51:25.590
then we're not gonna do it

00:51:25.590 --> 00:51:30.570
because this really requires
being in it for the long haul.

00:51:30.570 --> 00:51:34.500
And so just that one practice certainly

00:51:34.500 --> 00:51:39.500
has helped maintain my own
ability to stay in the work,

00:51:40.320 --> 00:51:42.063
to continue to show up.

00:51:43.980 --> 00:51:46.560
And another sort of principle,

00:51:46.560 --> 00:51:49.020
it's not just kind of talking about it

00:51:49.020 --> 00:51:52.350
and it's going from
teaching and talking about,

00:51:52.350 --> 00:51:55.050
but also to figuring
out how to practice it.

00:51:55.050 --> 00:51:58.560
But I love this idea of
shared responsibility

00:51:58.560 --> 00:51:59.790
for doing the work,

00:51:59.790 --> 00:52:03.120
and it truly has made it possible

00:52:03.120 --> 00:52:08.120
for me to still be a
social justice activist.

00:52:10.860 --> 00:52:13.830
So when I assumed the role

00:52:13.830 --> 00:52:17.130
of the first diversity
equity inclusion officer

00:52:17.130 --> 00:52:21.540
for McLean, what we
decided at the outset was,

00:52:21.540 --> 00:52:23.220
we're gonna do this,

00:52:23.220 --> 00:52:25.980
but we're gonna do it with a
model of shared responsibility.

00:52:25.980 --> 00:52:30.980
And what that means is,
it's not gonna be my job

00:52:31.710 --> 00:52:36.710
and it's not gonna be the job
of my, or the DEIO Office.

00:52:37.470 --> 00:52:39.810
The work isn't gonna be marginalized.

00:52:39.810 --> 00:52:43.770
It isn't going to be in parallel to.

00:52:43.770 --> 00:52:46.230
That has to be integrated.

00:52:46.230 --> 00:52:48.210
And that the whole organization,

00:52:48.210 --> 00:52:51.030
every member of the organization,

00:52:51.030 --> 00:52:55.260
needs to be challenged to
think about what their role is

00:52:55.260 --> 00:52:57.120
in promoting diversity.

00:52:57.120 --> 00:53:00.690
So really integrating it into.

00:53:00.690 --> 00:53:02.910
And it sounds like such a basic thing,

00:53:02.910 --> 00:53:05.010
like, yeah, of course,
shared responsibility.

00:53:05.010 --> 00:53:06.150
We're all responsible,

00:53:06.150 --> 00:53:09.573
but actually creating cultureship

00:53:09.573 --> 00:53:14.573
so that people feel like, okay,
I want to own responsibility

00:53:14.940 --> 00:53:16.680
for doing better.

00:53:16.680 --> 00:53:18.690
But then how do I do it?

00:53:18.690 --> 00:53:22.380
And creating a structure
and a system for doing that

00:53:22.380 --> 00:53:27.380
has absolutely been a personal
and professional lifesaver.

00:53:28.080 --> 00:53:32.760
Had I not done that, I'd have
burned out after year one.

00:53:32.760 --> 00:53:35.430
And that's not to say, as
I mentioned at the outset,

00:53:35.430 --> 00:53:36.960
it is a work in progress.

00:53:36.960 --> 00:53:38.700
I am definitely a work in progress.

00:53:38.700 --> 00:53:42.180
And in that first year,
I overextended myself

00:53:42.180 --> 00:53:44.523
in dozens of ways.

00:53:46.170 --> 00:53:48.300
And then another practice

00:53:48.300 --> 00:53:53.300
for sustaining has been
creating self-care partners.

00:53:53.670 --> 00:53:55.590
And sometimes, at the end of the week,

00:53:55.590 --> 00:53:58.620
in certain groups at work,

00:53:58.620 --> 00:54:01.680
we'll end the week with what
our commitment to self-care,

00:54:01.680 --> 00:54:04.890
one self-care activity is
gonna be over the weekend.

00:54:04.890 --> 00:54:07.587
And then we begin the
week by reporting back

00:54:07.587 --> 00:54:09.540
- Wow, that's great.
- And what that thing is.

00:54:09.540 --> 00:54:11.790
We also, I mentioned, we do checkouts.

00:54:11.790 --> 00:54:14.520
One thing that we do
as part of our checkout

00:54:14.520 --> 00:54:18.330
within our multicultural
psychology consultation team,

00:54:18.330 --> 00:54:20.520
our check in, did I say checkout?

00:54:20.520 --> 00:54:23.250
We check in.
(both laughs)

00:54:23.250 --> 00:54:24.710
Where that came from but...

00:54:26.322 --> 00:54:27.270
(indistinct talk)

00:54:27.270 --> 00:54:28.201
I know, I know, let's not, let's not,

00:54:28.201 --> 00:54:30.701
(both laughs)

00:54:31.820 --> 00:54:35.007
When we check in, we will
also rate level of burnout,

00:54:35.007 --> 00:54:38.190
and we do that routinely
on a scale of one to five,

00:54:38.190 --> 00:54:40.980
with five being the highest level,

00:54:40.980 --> 00:54:44.310
routinely, and it's just
been a really nice practice,

00:54:44.310 --> 00:54:49.310
one, 'cause it's makes
us, me specifically,

00:54:49.740 --> 00:54:52.593
pause and think, how am I doing?

00:54:53.910 --> 00:54:56.040
And integrating that practice,

00:54:56.040 --> 00:54:57.270
it's part of the agenda.

00:54:57.270 --> 00:54:58.890
It's not something that's hit or miss.

00:54:58.890 --> 00:55:01.860
We do it every single time we meet.

00:55:01.860 --> 00:55:03.900
What's the level of burnout?

00:55:03.900 --> 00:55:08.900
And then we ask, if the
level is three or higher,

00:55:09.870 --> 00:55:12.150
is there something you need from the group

00:55:12.150 --> 00:55:13.800
or the organization?

00:55:13.800 --> 00:55:14.820
Sometimes there is.

00:55:14.820 --> 00:55:17.137
Sometimes folks will say,

00:55:17.137 --> 00:55:19.680
"yeah, I need problem solving
around this one thing,

00:55:19.680 --> 00:55:21.600
or I need support on this."

00:55:21.600 --> 00:55:22.657
And other times people will say,

00:55:22.657 --> 00:55:24.630
"no, it just helped to be able to share

00:55:24.630 --> 00:55:27.717
that my burnout level
is creeping up there."

00:55:30.120 --> 00:55:32.280
Another practice that's
really helped sort of

00:55:32.280 --> 00:55:35.910
to keep me whole and
showing up in the work

00:55:35.910 --> 00:55:38.190
is one that we also instituted as part

00:55:38.190 --> 00:55:41.040
of that multicultural
psychology consultation team.

00:55:41.040 --> 00:55:44.160
And when we check in, we
report our level of burnout,

00:55:44.160 --> 00:55:46.310
but we also report on...

00:55:49.530 --> 00:55:54.530
upstander moments that we have
observed in the prior week,

00:55:55.440 --> 00:55:58.380
both seized,

00:55:58.380 --> 00:56:00.540
upstander moments where either you

00:56:00.540 --> 00:56:04.020
or you witness someone else really lean in

00:56:04.020 --> 00:56:08.130
and be effective as an
upstander in responding

00:56:08.130 --> 00:56:10.200
to an identity related aggression.

00:56:10.200 --> 00:56:13.140
And we also report on
missed opportunities.

00:56:13.140 --> 00:56:15.480
And sometimes those missed opportunities

00:56:15.480 --> 00:56:18.210
will be opportunities that, we ourselves.

00:56:18.210 --> 00:56:21.600
Like, this happened and I
really missed the moment,

00:56:21.600 --> 00:56:23.730
and I wanna name that.

00:56:23.730 --> 00:56:28.730
And that's been surprisingly
very rejuvenating.

00:56:29.160 --> 00:56:31.560
Often what happens in the group

00:56:31.560 --> 00:56:36.390
is that people will get
acknowledged for upstander moments

00:56:36.390 --> 00:56:40.200
that they didn't even
realize were so impactful.

00:56:40.200 --> 00:56:44.340
And so it's really positively
reinforcing the work.

00:56:44.340 --> 00:56:47.490
- Yeah, there's so many good
practical tips in there too.

